total() and type() are pure virtual functions in the base class and are overridden in every derived class. print() can be a normal function that calls the virtual functions.
A1.3 – Process All Bills Together (2 Marks)
Pseudocode:
ALGORITHM ProcessAllBills

BEGIN

    totalAmount ← 0

    FOR each bill in bills DO
        totalAmount ← totalAmount + bill.total()
        bill.print()
    END FOR

    DISPLAY "Total Hospital Billing Amount:", totalAmount

END


The array contains base-class pointers pointing to different derived bill objects. Runtime polymorphism ensures that the correct total() function is called for each bill.
Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
If a single Bill structure with a type field and switch statement were used instead of an abstract base class, each bill would store its type and all required billing data.
struct Bill {
    int type;
    double amount1, amount2, amount3;

    double total() {
        switch(type) {
            case 1:
                return amount1 + amount2;

            case 2:
                return amount1 + amount2 + amount3;

            case 3:
                return amount1;

            case 4:
                return amount1 + amount2;
        }
        return 0;
    }
};


Operation	Polymorphism	Switch-based design
Total time for n bills	O(n)	O(n)
Time per bill	O(1)	O(1)
Space complexity	O(n)	O(n)
Adding a new bill type	Add a new derived class	Modify the structure and switch
Maintainability	High	Lower as bill types increase

Conclusion: The polymorphic design is better because new bill types can be added by creating new derived classes without modifying the existing billing system. This follows the Open-Closed Principle.
